<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Konstantin Stanislavski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1863–1938</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Konstantin Stanislavski was a Russian actor and director who co-founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> Art <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> with Vladimir Nemirovich-Danchenko in 1898. His productions of Chekhov's plays made the theatre famous. He developed a system of actor training based on psychological truth, observation and inner experience, which shaped acting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and, through teachers such as Lee Strasberg, the method acting of American film and theatre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konstantin-stanislavski.jpg" alt="Konstantin Stanislavski" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantin_Stanislavski.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
